{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"minimalism\" \"origins\" \"art movement\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_430904731#2_383672660","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2407","rank":1,"snippet":"\"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_430904731#2_383672660\" , \"end_char\": 2667.000000 , \"headings\": \"Pop art Pop art Contents<cover>Origins Proto-pop United Kingdom: the Independent Group United States Early U.S. exhibitions France Spain Japan Italy Belgium Netherlands Russia Notable artists See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"In pop art, material is sometimes visually removed from its known context, isolated, or combined with unrelated material.. Amongst the early artists that shaped the pop art movement were Eduardo Paolozzi and Richard Hamilton in Britain, and Larry Rivers, Robert Rauschenberg and Jasper Johns among others in the United States. Pop art is widely interpreted as a reaction to the then-dominant ideas of abstract expressionism, as well as an expansion of those ideas. Due to its utilization of found objects and images, it is similar to Dada. Pop art and minimalism </cover>are considered to be art movements that precede postmodern art, or are some of the earliest examples of postmodern art themselves. Pop art often takes imagery that is currently in use in advertising. Product labeling and "}}
