{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196858521#3_2099638835","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a dedicated overview of the Minimalism movement and gives direct origin evidence: Minimalism emerged in New York in the early 1960s among artists renouncing recent art they saw as stale and academic. It directly supports an answer about where Minimalism came from.","learned":"TheArtStory states Minimalism emerged in early-1960s New York as artists deliberately rejected recent academic/stale art, supporting an art-historical rather than generic-creativity origin."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2408","reason":"This query found a highly relevant dedicated Minimalism source; continue through the results for more grade-3 origin evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2408"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196858521#4_2099640310","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2408","rank":3,"snippet":"fairly different whole.\" Summary of Minimalism Key Ideas & Accomplishments Overview of Minimalism Key Artists Carl Andre Dan Flavin Donald Judd Kenneth Noland Richard Serra Do Not Miss Post-Minimalism Light and Space American Art Important Art and Artists of Minimalism Die Fahne Hoch! (1959) Die (1962) Lever (1966) Useful Resources on Minimalism Books Share \" , \"segment\": \"It's important to me that I don't get my hands dirty. It's not because I'm instinctively lazy. It's a declaration: art is thought.\\\" 9 of 9 Summary of Minimalism<cover>Minimalism emerged in New York in the early 1960s </cover>among artists who were self-consciously renouncing recent art they thought had become stale and academic. A wave of new influences and rediscovered styles led younger artists to question conventional boundaries between various media. The new art favored the cool over the \"dramatic\": their sculptures were frequently fabricated from industrial materials and emphasized anonymity over the expressive excess of Abstract Expressionism. Painters and sculptors avoided overt symbolism and emotional content, but instead called attention to the materiality of the works. By the end of the 1970s, Minimalism had triumphed in America and Europe through a combination ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_195236522#3_218548923","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
