{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_195425182#2_218815639","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a dedicated Minimalism article and the passage directly states that minimalism in visual art emerged in New York in the early 1960s as artists moved toward geometric abstraction. It provides direct origin evidence for answering whether Minimalism comes simply from creativity.","learned":"A general Minimalism source states that visual-art Minimalism, also called minimal art/literalist art/ABC Art, emerged in early-1960s New York through artists moving toward geometric abstraction."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2408","reason":"The query continues to return direct, dedicated Minimalism-origin evidence; continue to harvest additional grade-3 sources if available."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2408"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_195425182#3_218818408","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2408","rank":6,"snippet":"and space 3.1 Concepts and design elements 3.2 Influences from Japanese tradition 3.3 Minimalist architects and their works 4 Literary minimalism 5 Minimal music 6 Minimalism in film 7 Software and UI design 7.1 Examples 8 Minimalism in science communication 9 See also 10 Notes 11 References 11.1 Citations 11.2 Sources 12 External links Minimal art, minimalism in visual art Main article: Minimalism (visual arts) Kazimir Malevich, Black Square, 1915, oil on canvas, 79.5 x 79.5 cm, Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow<cover>Minimalism in visual art, generally referred to as \"minimal art\", \"literalist art\" and \"ABC Art\" emerged in New York in the early 1960s </cover>as new and older artists moved toward geometric abstraction; exploring via painting in the cases of Nassos Daphnis, Frank Stella, Kenneth Noland, Al Held, Ellsworth Kelly, Robert Ryman and others; and sculpture in the works of various artists including David Smith, Anthony Caro, Tony Smith, Sol LeWitt, Carl Andre, Dan Flavin, Donald Judd and others. Judd's sculpture was showcased in 1964 at Green Gallery in Manhattan, as were Flavin's first fluorescent light works, while other leading Manhattan galleries like Leo Castelli Gallery and Pace Gallery also began ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_195236522#4_218551301","rank":5,"relevance":3}]}}
