{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"minimalism\" \"abstract expressionism\" \"1960s\" \"new york\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_33_493120401#0_1063194707","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2415","rank":1,"snippet":"artist population inspired by the European avant-garde, many of whom had relocated to the U.S. While Europe began the process of dealing with immeasurable trauma, New York emerged as a center of artistic activity, challenging Paris as the center of the international art world. Abstract Expressionism would emerge in the 1950s, followed by the rise of Neo-Dada, Pop, <cover>Minimalism, and Conceptual Art, among many other movements over the next decade. Rather than a style or set of ideas, Post-war American Art merely defines a time period, and is most often by auction houses to refer to art created between 1945 and 1970 (differentiating from Modern and Impressionist before and Contemporary after.) 1960s, United States, 1940s–1950s, Black-and-White Photography, New York Artists, Photography, Modern Photography, 20th Century Art, Modern and Impressionist Art, Abstract Expressionism</cover>Related Artists John Cage, Merce Cunningham, Stanley Kubrick, Barnett Newman, Richard Avedon, Agnes Martin, Dan Flavin, Ellsworth Kelly, Mark Rothko, Diane Arbus Filter by Sort Default Price (desc.) Price (asc.) Recently updated Recently added Artwork year (desc.) Artwork year (asc.) Reveal less Check Painting Check Photography Check Sculpture Check Prints Check Work on Paper Check Design Reveal more Reveal less Check"}}
