{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_755431544#4_1565564818","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to the history of Minimalism and directly explains its development: Minimalism became used in writing, painting, architecture, design, and creative fields, and Minimalists broke off from Abstract Expressionism by removing storytelling/metaphor and focusing on industrial materials, white space, and simplicity. This directly supports an answer about whether Minimalism comes from creativity by identifying its art/design historical sources and creative fields.","learned":"A history-of-Minimalism source says Minimalism arose across aesthetic, design, and creative fields and developed partly by breaking from Abstract Expressionism toward industrial materials, white space, and simplified works."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2428","reason":"This query produced a dedicated history-of-Minimalism result with direct evidence; continue to find more grade-3 historical sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2428"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_755431544#8_1565573174","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2428","rank":2,"snippet":"simplistic design made Judd a contributor to the art movement nonetheless. Carl Andre Another part of the group that began the New York movement of the 1960’s, Carl Andre helped to bring grid structures and a linear approach to sculptures. With simple visual elements in his approach to sculpture, he helped to further the new, more modern art genre that we know as minimal art today. Dan Flavin Dan Flavin, also a New York sculptor who moved from <cover>abstract expressionism to a new style of art experience that helped this modern movement. Unique due to his use of fluorescent lights in structures, his minimalistic art is still looked at today as one of the leaders in the history of minimalism. </cover>Summary of the History of Minimalism While there can be thousands of interpretations of what exactly minimalism means, understanding the history of the art movement and even the principles of thought behind it can help you to apply minimalism however you’d like. We all experience art, music, literature and design in different ways. Drawing on this history of the art that strips away any objects that are not essential can give you inspiration for minimalist living across a "}}
