{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_755431544#8_1565573174","relevance":3,"reason":"This is from a document dedicated to the history of Minimalism and directly discusses the 1960s New York movement, key figures such as Carl Andre and Dan Flavin, and the shift from Abstract Expressionism to a new minimal style. It provides direct support for where Minimalism came from.","learned":"A dedicated history source describes Minimalism as a 1960s New York movement shaped by artists such as Judd, Andre, and Flavin moving beyond Abstract Expressionism toward simplified, essential forms."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"minimalism\" \"started\" \"abstract expressionism\" \"industrial materials\")","reason":"Search for more dedicated history or design-history sources using the distinctive evidence that Minimalism started by breaking from Abstract Expressionism and using industrial materials."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"minimalism\" \"started\" \"abstract expressionism\" \"industrial materials\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_755431544#3_1565562361","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2429","rank":1,"snippet":"and reimagined the role art could play in society. By injecting creativity and thoughtfulness into the production of otherwise cheap and ubiquitous items, the Bauhaus school inspired the concept of elevated design for everyday life. 1960-1970: Minimalist Expansion The word minimalism itself gains popularity amongst certain groups of young artists in the 60s who resisted the stifling conventions of fine art (such as abstract painter Agnes Martin, sculptor and artist Donald Judd, artist Frank Stella and others). <cover>Minimalism started being used as a colloquial term in writing, painting, architecture, and other aesthetic, design, and creative fields. Minimalists started to break off from the Abstract Expressionism of previous generations, removing the storytelling or metaphors from their art and focusing on industrial materials, </cover>white space, and sleek, simplistic works. Moreover, in the 70s Dieter Rams introduced his principles of good design and ever since has been praised as one of the most influential figures in the pared back, user-friendly product design trend that’s so commonly called minimalist design. 1980-2000: Adaptation of Simple Living The next stage of the history of minimalism saw an ongoing increase in the simple art and simple living movements.\" , \"start_char"}}
