{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_510922120#10_945232902","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a Minimalism-focused source with a dedicated architecture section and direct evidence about where minimalist architecture comes from: traditional Japanese design, Bauhaus, De Stijl, Mies van der Rohe's 'less is more,' and later Zen-influenced Japanese architects. It directly supports an answer that Minimalism has specific art/design influences rather than simply coming from creativity in general.","learned":"Minimalist architecture is described as influenced by traditional Japanese design, Bauhaus, De Stijl, Mies van der Rohe, and later Zen Buddhism-influenced Japanese architects."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2440","reason":"This query produced a highly relevant Minimalism-focused source on architectural origins and influences; continue to find additional grade-3 evidence about where minimalist architecture/design comes from."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2440"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_43802617#1_112501278","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2440","rank":2,"snippet":"has continued to grow in popularity throughout the last century, but for every fan there is a maximalist critic who dismisses it as boring or sterile, lacking in imagination and heart. While <cover>minimalist architecture and product design can sometimes be cost-effective, eco-friendly, and may ultimately contribute to the democratization and accessibility of good design, it has also become synonymous with a rarified quest for the perfect object, a luxury that only the privileged few can afford and that can lead to its own form of perpetual excess. The Spruce / Michelle Becker Brief History of Minimalist Design Minimalist design emerged in the 20th century as a reaction to and rejection of the highly decorative styles of the past, from frilly Victorian architecture to Abstract Expressionist art. Some design historians trace the origins of minimalism to the simplified forms epitomized by the Dutch De Stijl movement of 1917 to the early 1930s and it is widely acknowledged to have been influenced by the zen simplicity of traditional Japanese </cover>gardens and interiors and the clean aesthetics of Scandinavian design. The less is more mantra of minimalist design is a quote from legendary German architect Mies Van Der Rohe, who in post-"}}
